{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-user feed limit (`max_feeds`, 0 = unlimited). Existing build, unit and integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself has not been exercised by any test I could run.\n\n- **Schema and model**: new migration adds `users.max_feeds int not null default 0`; the field is returned on the user object and added to the Go client's `User` and `UserModificationRequest`.\n- **Changing it**: `PUT /v1/users/{userID}` accepts `max_feeds` from administrators only. A non-admin who sends it gets 403; a negative value gets 400. It is written by a dedicated `SetUserMaxFeeds` storage call, and the general `UpdateUser` never touches the column, so the settings UI cannot overwrite it.\n- **Enforcement**: `storage.CreateFeed` now locks the user row, counts the user's feeds and inserts in one transaction, returning `ErrFeedLimitReached` when the user is at the limit. Every creation path goes through it (API, OPML import, web UI, Google Reader API), and concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit.\n- **`POST /v1/feeds`**: returns 400 and creates nothing when at the limit; it also checks before fetching the remote feed.\n- **`POST /v1/import`**: imports feeds until the limit is hit, then returns 400. The feeds imported before that point are kept, so an over-limit import is partial rather than all-or-nothing \u2014 matching how import already behaves on other mid-file errors.\n\nThings you might not expect:\n- Lowering `max_feeds` below a user's current feed count does not remove feeds; it only blocks new ones.\n- The limit message is English-only (no translation key was added to the locale files).\n- The 403 for non-admins applies even if they send the unchanged value.", "t": 1794461953.709}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794461953.709}
